# Real sell-quantity failure investigation

Actual runtime-03 round 2 diagnostic records `ValueError: V5 episode sell exceeds its causal open quantity` at diagnostics.py:448 for candidate policy `9b5ec4e75996d5593377142aa7ce057a357ccf0dc85be32a983f15db5c36a441`, quick window 2021-01-04 through 2021-03-31. The narrower draft changes the entry evaluator. The log does not contain the failing episode, ordered quantities, or oversell magnitude. It therefore establishes an accounting-validation failure, not its numerical cause.

Source evidence: core/backtest_engine.py stores position quantity as float; add-ons use `new_quantity = current_qty + quantity` at 5352, scale-outs subtract floats at 5760, and final close uses the remaining float at 5825. `_record_v5_fill` preserves `fill.quantity` at 5921 rather than using the six-decimal transaction display. In core/pit_optimizer_v5/diagnostics.py, `_d` reconstructs Decimal from str(value); `_validate_episode_quantity_path` accumulates those decimal representations and rejects any strictly larger sell at 447 before its existing six-decimal quantity-path and total-buy/sell reconciliation. Binary floating-point subtraction/addition can differ from exact decimal reconstruction, so precision dust is a plausible explanation. Source review does not establish that this actual failure is dust rather than a material oversell. No split quantity adjustment was found in the reviewed quantity mutation paths; this does not establish the actual episode's corporate-action history.

No source fix is justified from the available actual log. Preserve the strict causal invariant until the actual offending quantities are known. The minimal next diagnostic is to augment this existing failure's exception text with episode ID, fill index, session, action/reason, exact `str(quantity)`, `str(cumulative_quantity)`, their difference, the recorded change.quantity_after, and the preceding recorded quantity_after. For complete attribution, collect the ordered BUY/SELL quantities and quantity_path for that actual episode on the same real evaluation. This is diagnostic instrumentation only: keep the guard and failure status unchanged, emit no success/performance substitute, and capture stderr during the real run because the container log driver is none. Decimal differences should be compared with the corresponding actual float transitions before deciding whether to canonicalize quantities or correct an engine oversell.

`core/pit_optimizer_v5/diagnostics.py` and `core/backtest_engine.py` are evaluator source files; changing either requires notifying the controller and rebuilding/resealing the evaluator image. Neither was changed here. No compilation or Ruff was necessary because no source changed. Only this report was written. No tests, probes, application imports/execution, Docker/network calls, runtime artifact writes, staging, hook changes, or commits occurred. Frozen actual failure remains unchanged.

## Authorized numeric diagnostic enhancement

The controller subsequently authorized a permanent error-detail enhancement only. Changed `core/pit_optimizer_v5/diagnostics.py` at the existing causal oversell guard to include fill index, sell/open/excess quantities, their existing `_q` normalizations, current quantity_after, and preceding quantity_after when available. Each value is capped at 96 characters; the fixed nine-field error remains below the container entry 2048-character limit. No symbols, issuers, source content, or environment values are included. Missing predecessor and unavailable numeric normalization use None; normalization arithmetic errors cannot replace the existing causal ValueError. The guard, quantity arithmetic, accepted evidence, and failure outcome remain unchanged.

Source SHA256: `9811e16dc10066c473f5369edd691e73a8a76cd83d94b71a3c3dbeeaba34dd01`. Scoped Ruff, in-memory compile without application imports, and diff whitespace check passed. This source is in evaluator closure, requiring controller-owned image rebuild/reseal before the separate real diagnostic run. No baseline reruns, tests, probes, application/Docker/network execution, runtime writes, staging, hooks, or commits were performed.

## Actual evidence and narrow precision correction

Read the completed actual run `development/runs/narrower-quantity-diagnostic-244f746cbf6e-01/console.log`. At fill index 2 it records sell `297.4124892723657`, reconstructed open `297.41248927236568`, excess `2E-14`, normalized sell/open `297.412489`, normalized excess `0.000000`, quantity_after `0.0`, and previous_quantity_after `297.4124892723657`. This establishes a closing binary-float remainder differing from Decimal reconstruction by numerical dust.

The guard now permits an apparent excess only for an explicit exit transition with zero quantity_after, when converting sell and reconstructed open back to float gives exact equality AND their existing six-decimal quantizations agree. This is more conservative than accepting every excess below a fixed 0.000001: the numbers must lie in the same binary-float rounding interval and the same decimal quantization cell (difference less than 0.000001), and the closing path must agree. First-fill SELL always still rejects. Material oversells and mismatched closing quantities still raise the bounded numeric diagnostic. No quantities are clipped, replaced, or dropped: the exact Decimal subtraction remains, and all existing per-fill quantity-path, action, ordering, duplicate, aggregate buy/sell, and terminal reconciliation checks remain unchanged. The residual rounds to zero under the existing quantity evidence contract.

Final diagnostics.py SHA256: `ba350e2bcc71c9fd67d48419d7f24ae6c15ee53e89f575b4398e9aa80bcc3f35`. Scoped Ruff, in-memory compilation, and diff whitespace checks passed. This evaluator-closure change requires controller-owned rebuild/reseal and a real rerun. Only diagnostics.py and this report changed for this increment; no tests, probes, application execution, runtime artifact edits, staging, hooks, or commits.
